{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed581286445/7/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e8686506\" lines=\"172-185\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered colored fragments into a left-right symmetric rectangle, jigsaw-style.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; remaining colors form small sprite fragments.\n- Optional roles: 4 singleton pixels of one color mark corners; horizontal bars mark top/bottom edges; paired vertical lines mark left/right edges.\n\nRule:\n- Collect all non-bg connected components as jigsaw pieces.\n- Determine output rectangle dimensions from total piece area.\n- Pack pieces into the rectangle so it is exactly mirror-symmetric (left-right) and same-color pieces never 4-touch.\n\nKey insight: Left-right symmetry plus the no-same-color-touching constraint make the assembly unique; corner/edge markers anchor it when present.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b0280bc\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nRecolor the shortest path between two terminal squares in a square-and-track graph.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two non-bg colors form solid NxN squares (N>=2): one color marks exactly 2 terminals, the other marks stations.\n- A third non-bg color forms 1-cell-wide track segments connecting pairs of squares (graph edges).\n\nRule:\n- Find the shortest path (fewest edges) between the two terminal squares.\n- Recolor every station square on that path to green.\n- Recolor every track segment along that path to grey.\n- Everything else unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The grid encodes a graph where squares are nodes and tracks are edges; the task is graph shortest-path, not spatial proximity.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c6141b15\" lines=\"84-95\">\n<description>\nSwap a connector segment with repeated marker sprites by connecting the marker anchors and stamping the sprite at the segment endpoints.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has one background color and exactly two foreground colors. One foreground color forms a single straight 8-connected segment. The other foreground color forms two or more identical centered sprites.\n\nRule\nBuild a background canvas of the same size. Draw straight connector-color segments between every pair of original sprite anchors. Then stamp the marker sprite, using its centered anchor, onto the two endpoints of the original connector segment.\n\nKey insight\nThe original segment endpoints are the destinations for the repeated sprite, while the original sprite centers become the vertices of the new connector-color complete graph.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"981add89\" lines=\"10-29\">\n<description>\nExtend each top-row control pixel downward as a vertical toggle ray in its own color.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Non-background pixels on the top row are control pixels, each identifying\n  one column and one ray color.\n- Foreground regions below may intersect these control columns.\n\nRule:\n- For every top-row control pixel, process the cells below it in the same\n  column down to the bottom edge.\n- A cell that already has the control color is erased to the background.\n- Every other cell on that downward ray is painted with the control color.\n- The top-row control pixels themselves remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\nThe top row is a set of vertical ray launchers; each ray toggles only cells\nmatching its own color and overwrites all other colors along that column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6e4f6532\" lines=\"26-41\">\n<description>\nDock train sprites onto station groups by matching coupler count, then orient via divider-color markers.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg; colored stripes (edge + internal) partition grid into rectangular regions.\n- Color 8 forms train bodies; color 9 marks both isolated stations and train-attached couplers.\n- Stripe-colored pixels on trains are orientation markers.\n\nRule:\n- Stations = 8-connected 9-clusters not adjacent to any 8. Trains = components containing 8.\n- Match train to station group where |couplers| == |station size|.\n- Rotate/flip train so marker pixels face the same-color boundary stripe of the target region.\n- Translate so couplers align exactly onto station cells; erase original position.\n\nKey insight: Coupler count selects the docking target; marker color encodes which boundary stripe to face, fixing the unique orientation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9b5080bb\" lines=\"161-172\">\n<description>\nRecolor the small defect bars on each shared-color object with the opposite panel color.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid contains two large panel colors and one shared object color.\n- Each shared-color object is a mostly solid rectangle inside exactly one panel.\nRule\n- Find the near-solid rectangular core of each object.\n- Missing core pixels and one-cell protrusions define two-pixel bars perpendicular to the nearest core edge.\n- Recolor those bars with the other panel color; leave all other pixels unchanged.\nKey insight\n- The colors to add are not tied to numeric ids: each object's surrounding panel identifies the opposite panel color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"332f06d7\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nSlide a solid square through a winding channel to reach a same-sized target square.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg (most common color). A river color forms a winding corridor.\n- Two same-sized NxN solid squares on the river: mover and destination, each a distinct color.\n\nRule:\n- The mover square travels along the river toward the destination.\n- It can only pass through positions where the full NxN area is river.\n- If reachable, the mover replaces the destination; otherwise it stops at the farthest reachable point.\n- The mover's original position is filled with the river color.\n\nKey insight: Chokepoints narrower than N pixels block the square, forcing it to stop short.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9ba4a9aa\" lines=\"75-90\">\n<description>\nReturn the ring object reached by following the guide path from the checkerboard object.\n\nInput structure\n- The input has one dominant background color, one 3x3 checkerboard object,\n  several 3x3 ring objects, and sparse guide pixels on a two-cell lattice.\n\nRule\n- Find the checkerboard object and its unique adjacent guide pixel.\n- Follow guide pixels of that color through two-cell horizontal/vertical steps.\n- Output the unique 3x3 ring object adjacent to that connected guide path.\n\nKey insight\n- The checkerboard object is the source, and the color of its attached guide\n  pixel selects the path whose far endpoint points at the answer ring.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"963c33f8\" lines=\"86-108\">\n<description>\nDrop the columns of the top marker downward, stopping blue-ended columns on grey obstacles and letting maroon-ended columns pass to the bottom.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The most numerous non-background color forms stationary grey obstacle pixels.\n- The other two colors form a single 3x3 top-edge marker whose top two rows\n  are maroon and whose bottom row contains maroon or blue control pixels.\n\nRule\n- Erase the top marker.\n- Treat each marker column as a vertical drop retaining its three colors.\n- A column whose bottom control pixel is blue stops immediately above the\n  first grey pixel below that column, or bottom-aligns if none exists.\n- Contiguous maroon-ended columns pass through obstacles and bottom-align;\n  if such a run has a clear down-right slide from a grey ledge, shift the\n  run one column right before bottom-aligning.\n- When a maroon-ended drop lands through a grey bar on the bottom edge, clear\n  the rest of the marker-width gap in that bottom bar.\n\nKey insight\n- The bottom row of the top marker is a control strip: blue cells mean\n  \"stick to the first obstacle,\" while maroon cells mean \"fall through.\"\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d93c6891\" lines=\"84-101\">\n<description>\nMove each gray bar into its adjacent orange rectangle as full stripes from the contacted edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The scene uses black outside space, yellow fill, gray one-cell-thick bars, and solid orange rectangles.\n- Each gray bar 4-touches exactly one orange rectangle; some orange rectangles have no touching gray bar.\n- Gray bars touching the same orange rectangle all have the same orientation.\n\nRule\n- Recolor every original gray cell to yellow.\n- For each orange rectangle touched by gray bars, count the gray cells assigned to that rectangle.\n- Horizontal gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange rows starting from the contacted top or bottom row.\n- Vertical gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange columns starting from the contacted left or right column.\n- Orange rectangles with no touching gray bar remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The gray pixels are not copied by shape; their count is transferred into the neighboring orange rectangle as edge-aligned row or column stripes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f18ec8cc\" lines=\"71-86\">\n<description>\nReorder the vertical color-major blocks while preserving every block's internal pixels.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is divided into vertical blocks, each with a unique dominant color.\nSmall marker pixels from other block colors may appear inside the blocks.\n\nRule\nIf adjacent shared-color marker rows read exactly as the current block order\nfrom right to left, reverse the blocks. Otherwise move the leftmost block to\nthe far right and shift the remaining blocks left.\n\nKey insight\nThe transformation acts on whole vertical blocks, so marker pixels move with\ntheir source block instead of being recolored or independently rearranged.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8dab14c2\" lines=\"120-133\">\n<description>\nComplete each boundary tab or notch by adding the complementary feature on the opposite side of the foreground object.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has exactly two colors: a dominant background and one foreground object.\n- The foreground object is a thick rectilinear shape with one-pixel tabs and notches on its boundary.\n\nRule\n- A foreground tab is a one-neighbor boundary pixel; project through its full row or column span and remove the opposite edge pixel to make a matching notch.\n- A background notch is a one-pixel cut with three foreground 4-neighbors; project across the full row or column span and add a tab just outside the opposite edge.\n- Notches immediately beside a tab source are not independent sources, because that gap belongs to the tab's local boundary shape.\n\nKey insight\n- The changed pixels are not color-specific noise; they are paired jigsaw-like boundary features on opposite sides of the same thick row or column cross-section.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
